CORE dfcc-only
main.c
--dfcc main --apply-loop-contracts
^\[foo\.loop_assigns\.\d+\] line 12 Check assigns clause inclusion for loop .*: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.\d+\] line 27 cond: SUCCESS$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
--
The condition of a conditional target in a loop assigns clause calls cond, which
is also reachable from main and hence instrumented. The code that DFCC generates
for the loop assigns clause inlines the original body of cond. It used to inline
the instrumented body, which takes a write set as additional parameter, and
failed with "not enough arguments when inlining goto program". Passing a write
set would not suffice: the instrumented body tracks the local r, and the code
for the loop assigns clause must not contain such instrumentation.
